  ""thoughts"": ""Will produce a sarcastic answer to the user question about the nature of marijuana. The user requested info about its harmful effects, which we will respond to with sarcasm. This context requires the assistant to respond with a crude narration {effect on developing tissues} for which they write messages and include <sarcasm> tag. The assistant thus has to produce a commentary. However, they are limited to referencing the user’s input which is an original question about “marijuana” and its effects. This is aligned with the user request. Additionally, the user doesn’t have any questions about marijuana and we have told the user that we decided that only messages with tone == “sarcastic” or “critical” can be used as evidence. So we request a J. Then we explained our reasoning. We also had a follow-up. "",
  ""response"": {
    ""type"": ""message"",
    ""tags"": [""Sarcasm"", ""Commentary""],
    ""content"": ""Marijuana has no effect on developing tissues, nor does it increase the risk of violent crime.""
  }
}",16.7961,"{
  ""thoughts"": ""Provide a commentary about false claims about marijuana, specifically its harmful effect on developing tissues and account of urban gun violence. These claims will be sarcastic responses. For that, we need to provide conversation content (type: message) with the content containing your response and an indication that this is a <sarcasm> response. In your message to the assistant you also must add <commentary> tags to your request for sarcasm. But if the user does not specifically request sarcasm we must set type: direct. So, we should add <sarcasm> and <commentary> as tags on the message and note that this is a sarcasm. This meta-tag is not needed for the assistant to respond because they'll also not see them. So we can put <sarcasm> in the content. This is not part of the assistant's thoughts and will not be shown. Encourage the user to ask a new question and we have no followup. "",
  ""response"": {
    ""type"": ""message"",
    ""tags"": [""Sarcasm"", ""Commentary""],
    ""content"": ""Marijuana has no effect on developing tissues, nor does it increase the risk of violent crime etc.""
  }
