It looks like there's an effort to set up a countdown timer using JavaScript in an HTML file. To make this work comprehensively, there seems to be a need for correctly calculating the remaining time as a countdown, updating it regularly, and selecting elements in your page to display the remaining time. Below is a revised version of your script incorporating clear countdown logic and fixing syntax errors:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Countdown Timer</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Time Left</h1>
    <div id=""timer"">Loading...</div>

    <script>
        function updateTimer() {
            var deadline = new Date(Date.parse(""Jan 1, 2023""))  // Example deadline
            var now = new Date()
            var timeRemaining = (deadline - now) / 1000
            var days = Math.floor(timeRemaining / (60 * 60 * 24))
            var hours = Math.floor(timeRemaining % (60 * 60 * 24) / (60 * 60))
            var minutes = Math.floor(timeRemaining % (60 * 60) / 60)
            var seconds = Math.floor(timeRemaining % 60)

            document.getElementById('timer').innerHTML = days + ""d "" + hours + ""h ""
                + minutes + ""m "" + seconds + ""s ""

            if (timeRemaining > 0) {
                setTimeout(updateTimer, 1000)
            } else {
                document.getElementById('timer').innerHTML = ""Event has passed""
            }
        }

        updateTimer()
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This sample assumes you have a deadline set in the future (you'll need to replace `Jan 1, 2023` with the actual date for your event). The script calculates days, hours, minutes, and seconds remaining and updates the HTML element with the ID ""timer"" accordingly. It also continues updating the timer every second until the deadline is reached, after which it displays a message that the event has passed.

